        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state television (IRIB) and Tasnim News Agency framed the MOU expiration on 2026-08-17 as a US failure, presenting the lapse without renewal as evidence that the Trump administration had not honored its own framework commitments. IRNA published a Foreign Ministry statement in the same news cycle describing the expired authorization as a unilateral US mechanism and declining to announce countermeasures. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Kayhan editorial coverage on 2026-08-18 cited the Hormuz dark-fleet data as confirmation that Iran's maritime posture had made external pressure economically self-defeating, framing the parallel shipping flow as a form of sovereign financial resilience. Press TV broadcast a segment on 2026-08-19 showing Iranian tanker deliveries to unnamed Asian customers alongside the dark-fleet AIS-data, without sourcing the underlying Lloyd's List analysis. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Supreme Leader Khamenei's office had not published a statement on the sanctions expiration or the MOU's lapse as of press time. IRNA and Tasnim both cycled Baghaei's 2026-08-11 statement — rejecting Trump's compensation demand — across multiple broadcast slots without new material updates in the 48 hours preceding this report. State-media coverage of the Israeli Channel 12 polling data (2026-08-18) centered on Netanyahu's electoral vulnerability rather than the military dimension. No hardline press editorial called for a resumption of direct IRGC or Hezbollah operations as of press time. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

